---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import Gloss from '../../components/Gloss.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const one = assertion('the-one-election-it-gave-a-democrat')
const split = assertion('one-democrat-carried-it-and-seven-did-not')
const turnout = assertion('the-presidential-premium-is-twenty-three-points')

const spec = (a: typeof one, yTitle: string) =>
  bar(
    a.figures.map((f) => f.label),
    a.figures.map((f) => f.value),
    { yTitle, labelValues: true },
  )

const oneSpec = spec(one, 'Republican share of the two-party vote')
const splitSpec = spec(split, 'Democratic share of the two-party vote')
const turnoutSpec = spec(turnout, 'Per cent of registered voters')
---

<Article slug="the-one-democrat">
  <p>
    On 7 November 2006, 37,605 people in Allen County went to one of its 139 polling divisions and
    filled in a ballot with eight partisan offices on it, two seats on the Ohio Supreme Court and
    four statewide questions. At the top of it they voted for Ted Strickland, a Democrat, 18,000 to
    J. Kenneth Blackwell's 17,184.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={one} open />
  <ChartFigure
    id="gov"
    title="Allen County's vote for governor, 2002 to 2018"
    caption="Republican share of the two major-party candidates. 2002, 2014 and 2018 from county-grain files; 2006 and 2010 summed from the Ohio Secretary of State's own precinct workbooks. No file exists for 2022."
    columns={['Year', 'Republican share']}
    rows={one.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="gov" set:html={JSON.stringify(oneSpec)} is:inline />
  <Gloss>
    Five of these elections were sitting in a repository this corpus already cites, in a directory
    listing that had been read for one year out of twelve. The cost shows: the 2018 page here said
    the county's partisan balance barely moves and that who turns out matters more than how they
    vote. That was drawn from three consecutive ballots. Across five it moves twenty-five points.
  </Gloss>

  <h2>One ballot, eight answers</h2>
  <p>
    What makes 2006 strange is not that the county went Democratic once. It is that it went
    Democratic once <em>on a ballot where it went Republican seven times</em>. Sherrod Brown lost
    Allen County by 2,924 votes the same night he won Ohio by twelve points. Marc Dann lost it by
    5,365. Jim Jordan carried it by 7,252.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={split} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="split"
    title="Democratic share by office, Allen County, 2006"
    caption="Share of the two major-party candidates in each race. The eight offices, in order: governor 51.2, U.S. Senate 45.9, treasurer 44.3, secretary of state 44.1, attorney general 42.6, state representative 41.8, auditor 41.6, U.S. representative 40.2."
    columns={['Office', 'Democratic share']}
    rows={split.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="split" set:html={JSON.stringify(splitSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    The widest margin on the whole ballot went to neither party. Robert Cupp, running for the Ohio
    Supreme Court on the nonpartisan section of the ticket, took 25,564 votes here against Ben
    Espy's 9,127 — 73.7 per cent, better than any partisan candidate in the county. Cupp is from
    Lima. Fourteen years later he would appear on the same county's ballot again, running unopposed
    for its seat in the state House.
  </p>
  <p>
    Below the offices sat four statewide questions, and the county said yes to exactly one of them —
    54.6 per cent for the fifth, against 46.6, 43.5 and 34.9 for the others. Two of the four became
    law that December: a statewide smoking prohibition, which the Revised Code still records as
    <em>enacted by initiative</em>, and a minimum wage written into the Ohio Constitution. Which
    numbered question was which, the workbook does not say and nothing else here does either. The
    obvious move is to match the county's one yes to the one statute, and that would be a guess
    wearing a citation.
  </p>

  <h2>Who shows up</h2>
  <p>
    The same workbooks carry something the later files drop: how many people were registered and how
    many actually voted. Three elections can now be measured that way, and two of them are midterms.
  </p>
  <AssertionCard assertion={turnout} />
  <ChartFigure
    id="turnout"
    title="Turnout in Allen County"
    caption="Ballots cast as a share of registered voters. 2006: 37,605 of 68,797. 2010: 33,867 of 69,931, checked against the file's own county-totals row. 2020: 48,353 of 67,374."
    columns={['Election', 'Turnout']}
    rows={turnout.figures.map((f) => [f.label, `${f.value.toFixed(1)}%`])}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="turnout" set:html={JSON.stringify(turnoutSpec)} is:inline />
  <p>
    The registration roll behaved like the county. It stood at 69,931 in 2010 and 67,374 in 2020, a
    fall of 3.66 per cent, against a population that fell 3.88 per cent over the same decade. What
    did not hold steady was the number of places to vote: 139 precincts in 2006, 121 in 2010, and 88
    from 2014 on. The county cut better than a third of its polling divisions in eight years while
    losing four per cent of its voters.
  </p>
  <p>
    None of this reaches 2022 or 2024. The volunteer repository has no Ohio file for either year at
    any grain, and the office that certified the returns answers an automated request with a
    maintenance page. The most recent contest for governor readable here is seven years old.
  </p>
</Article>
